%[text] %[text:anchor:H_327926B0] ## Introduction
%[text] In this interactive course, you will learn how to use Arduino technology to measure and analyze your reaction times. Working with LEDs and buttons will give you hands-on experience in accurately measuring your reaction time. Furthermore, you will have the opportunity to compare the reaction times of your dominant and non-dominant hands, providing valuable insights into the influence of hand dominance on your reaction speed. Get ready to dive into the captivating world of reaction analysis and discover the fascinating aspects of your own reflexes!
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3D1C0535] ## Measuring reaction time
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B846371E] ### Wiring diagram
%[text] In this part of the script, we are starting to use a push button to gather user feedback. The Arduino board will be used to measure the user's reaction time. As a first step, we will wire a simple circuit with one LED and one push button. The Simulink model provided will be used to turn on the LED randomly, and the button will be pushed to turn off the LED. The time the user takes to make the button after the LED turns on is considered the reaction time. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Four leg push button diagram](text:image:7ae7)
%[text]{"align":"center"} Figure 1 - Four leg push button diagram - this image was created using [fritzing-parts](https://github.com/fritzing/fritzing-parts/)
%[text] In Figure 1, we show how the push button functions. It is important to note that your push button might differ slightly from the one presented here. This button is a **normally open** button, meaning the circuit is open when it is released in its normal state. Pushing the button closes the circuit, creating a connection between the right and left sides of the button. Another push button could be normally closed or make a connection between the top and bottom pins. You should check your button's specs before continuing and adapt the rest of this script if you use a different button.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![ Wiring diagram one button](text:image:9fee)
%[text]{"align":"center"} Figure 2 - Wiring diagram one button - this image was created using [fritzing-parts](https://github.com/fritzing/fritzing-parts/)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F819B4BD] #### How does the button work?
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:9a37) **Exercise.** Based on the Figures 1 and 2, what should be the voltage at the analog pin read from the button when the button is UNPUSHED?
Exercise1(0) %[control:dropdown:4909]{"position":[11,12]}
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:9286) **Exercise.** Based on the two previous figures, what should be the voltage at the analog pin read from the button when the button is PUSHED?
Exercise2(0) %[control:dropdown:0cad]{"position":[11,12]}
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:54fc) **Exercise.** Select the board and pins that you are currently using.
MyBoard = "Arduino Uno"; %[control:dropdown:3214]{"position":[11,24]}
MyLEDPin = ""; %[control:editfield:0eda]{"position":[12,14]}
MyButtonPin = ""; %[control:editfield:9853]{"position":[15,17]}
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:8d45) **Try.** Open the `OneButtonTest.slx` to test your wiring. The pin number associated with the LED and Button should be set to the correct value.
  %[control:button:6eb3]{"position":[1,2]}
SetButtonModel(MyBoard,MyLEDPin,MyButtonPin);
%[text] 
%[text] ![Simulink icon](text:image:1958) **Pro-tip.** The wiring is correct if the scope changes from 0 to 1 when the button is pushed. If your LED is not turning on, it is potentially because the polarity of the LED is reversed. For more details on how to wire an LED, please refer to [BlinkToSOS.m](file:BlinkToSOS.m:M_C39BF5DB).
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:7b41) **Try.**  Click **Run with IO** in the Simulink toolstrip and observe the LED and scope when pushing the button. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_CD2D6D81] ### 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7245CB49] ### Two button experiment
%[text] In the previous section, we learned how to use push buttons. In this section, we will design an experiment with two button to explore the following hypothesis:
%[text]  **"The reaction time in one's dominant hand is faster than in a non-dominant hand."**
%[text] The reaction time will be measured using two buttons and two LEDs. The experiment will go as follows:
%[text] 1. One of the LEDs will turn on randomly.
%[text] 2. The user must push the corresponding button (left or right) to turn off the LED.
%[text] 3. The reaction time is considered to be the time taken to turn off the LED.
%[text] 4. This reaction time will be registered and transmitted to the computer through USB connection for analysis. \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D39E2E6E] #### Wiring
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:903b) **Exercise.** Based on the wiring from the previous section, wire a second button and LED to your Arduino. When done, use the following button to show the correct wiring diagram.
ShowWiringTwoButton(Enable = false,HighlightWiring = false,ExternalFigure = false) %[control:checkbox:6310]{"position":[30,35]} %[control:checkbox:7103]{"position":[54,59]} %[control:checkbox:292a]{"position":[77,82]}
%[text] In Figure 3, the blue wire represents the ground (GND), the red wire represents the board voltage (either 3.3 or 5V, depending on the board), and the green wire means the connection to the digital pins.
%[text] We provide an updated Simulink model called [`TwoButtonReaction.slx`](file:../Models/TwoButtonReaction.slx) `to gather the reaction time using two buttons.`
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:1094) **Try.** Open the model and modify the pin number accordingly.
  %[control:button:023c]{"position":[1,2]}

% Open the model:
open(fullfile("Models","TwoButtonReaction.slx"))

% Programmatically set the board:
Mdl = load_system(fullfile("Models","TwoButtonReaction")); 
set_param(Mdl,"HardwareBoard", MyBoard); 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2178198A] #### Gather Data
%[text] Now that you have prepared the model to run on the Arduino board, it is time to deploy it and gather reaction time data using your Arduino.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:5e22) **Try.** Deploy the model to your Arduino by clicking the **Build, Deploy & Start** button in the Simulink toolstrip (**HARDWARE TAB**)
%%
%[text] The data is gathered through a serial port connection: every time the hardware collects a reaction time, it is communicated through the Arduino USB board. This module contains an app that can monitor the data sent by your Arduino through the USB port.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:9f06) **Try.** Open the app.
  %[control:button:585d]{"position":[1,2]}
evalc("SerialMonitor"); % App is opened using evalc to avoid screenshot of the app to appear in script.
%%
%[text] Now that we have deployed the model to your Arduino hardware and opened the app, we can start gathering the reaction time. 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:0c2a) **Try.** Gather reaction time following these steps:
%[text] 1. Select the **COM port** corresponding to your Arduino Hardware.
%[text] 2. Click **Connect**.
%[text] 3. Click **Start/Stop**.
%[text] 4. Push both buttons at the same time to turn them off.
%[text] 5. Please turn off the LED by pushing the button every time it turns on.
%[text] 6. *( Repeat many times )*
%[text] 7. When enough reaction time is measured, click **Start/Stop** again.
%[text] 8. Click **Export Data** to save the reaction time in a mat file. \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_CD7EAEC5] ## Lab report
%[text] In this section of the scripts, you will explore processing real-world data you just gathered using your Arduino. You will learn how to pre-process, visualize, and analyze the data. For those of you who did not collect reliable data or decided to skip the previous section, we are providing our reaction data in **`Data/ReactionTwoButton.mat`**.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8C381E3A] ### Pre-processing raw data
%[text] Pre-processing data is a crucial step in data analysis as it helps ensure the information's accuracy, reliability, and relevance. By removing outliers, handling missing values, normalizing data, and addressing other data quality issues, you will obtain more meaningful results and make informed decisions based on reliable data. Before getting started, you should load the data that you have gathered through your Arduino. If you cannot acquire reliable data, we are also providing results from our own experiment in **`Data/ReactionTwoButton.mat`**.
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:6043) **Exercise.** Select the data you want to use to perform the data analysis.
load("Data/ReactionTwoButton.mat","SerialData") %[control:filebrowser:5466]{"position":[6,34]}

% Check that the data loaded is from your arduino
if exist("SerialData","var") == 1
    disp("Good job! You loaded data gathered from the Arduino board.")
    SerialData
else
    warning("The data you loaded does not include serialdata gathered from your Arduino.")
end
%[text] The SerialData variable that was just loaded is a table containing two vectors:
%[text] - Data: correspond to the reaction time that was gathered using the Arduino. Positive reaction time corresponds to a reaction time from the right button, and negative reaction time correspon to a reaction time collected from the left button.
%[text] - Time: correspond to the time at which the reaction time was collected. \
%%
%[text] The value column represents the reaction time gathered from the Arduino in milliseconds. The value is positive if the reaction time if it is the right LED reaction and negative if it is the left side reaction time. To make the table more readable, we should add a column with "left" and "right" and only keep the absolute value of the value column.
%[text] ![MATLAB icon](text:image:2acf)  If you are unfamiliar with using MATLAB Table, vector and logical indexing, consider completing [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted), a 2-hours training
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:5c8c) **Try.** Create a logical vector with value `true` if the data was gathered from the left button and `false` otherwise.
 IsRight = SerialData.Data > 0;
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:2634) **Exercise.** Select the right operator to define the logical vector with `true` value if the data was gathered from the left button and `false` otherwise.
IsLeft = SerialData.Data == 0; %[control:dropdown:4b24]{"position":[26,28]}

% Check Answer:
IsLeft = CheckIsLeft(IsLeft,SerialData);
%%
%[text] Now that we have identified which reaction time are from the right or left hand, we can add a column to the table storing this information. With this extra column, the sign of the reaction time becomes redundant and can be omitted. This increases the readability of the data. 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:3f1d) **Try.** Create a column "`Side"` indicating 
  %[control:button:7bb3]{"position":[1,2]}

if ~isempty(IsLeft) % Check that the IsLeft vector was defined correctly
    SerialData.Side(IsRight) = "Right"; % Add column with "Left" and "Right" based on the vector
    SerialData.Side(IsLeft) = "Left";   % defined previously
    SerialData.Side = categorical(SerialData.Side); % Turn the "Left" and "Right" into categorical
    SerialData % Display the table
end
%[text] In the previous code, we are transforming the Side column as a [categorical](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/categorical-arrays.html) datatype. Categorical data types are used to store data with values from a finite set of discrete categories. In our case, because the side can only be "Left" or "Right", using categorical enables better performance and less memory usage than if we were using [string](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/characters-and-strings.html) datatype.
%%
%[text] Now that we created a `Side` column in the SerialData timetable, we do not need to keep the sign in the `Data column.` 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:0a1d) **Try.** Run this code to keep the absolute value of the reaction time.
SerialData.Data = abs(SerialData.Data) % Keep absolute value of the reaction time
%%
%[text] Before ro proceed with a formal data analysis, we should create some quick data visualization.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:90d1) **Try.** Select the Side to visualize the histogram of reaction time.
Side = "None"; %[control:dropdown:2575]{"position":[8,14]}

switch (Side)
    case "Right"
        figure
        histogram(SerialData.Data(SerialData.Side == Side),SeriesIndex=1)
        title("Reaction time on right hand")
        ylabel("Reaction time (ms)")
        xlabel("Count")
    case "Left"
        figure
        histogram(SerialData.Data(SerialData.Side == Side),SeriesIndex=2)
        title("Reaction time on left hand")
        ylabel("Reaction time (ms)")
        xlabel("Count")
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3BBFD5AD] ### Data analysis
%[text] In this section, we will perform a simple statistical analysis of the data gathered using the Arduino board. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_957E667F] #### Create box chart
%[text] A box chart (or box plot) displays statistical information (median, lower/upper quartiles, outliers) about the plotted data. They will help us compare the data from the right and left sides.
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:7658) **Exercise.** Use a **Create Plot** Live Task it to create a box chart over the **`SerialData`** with the **Side** as X-data and **Data** as Y-data
%[task:5691] %[task:5691]
legend("off")
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:2264) **Exercise.** Modify the axis labels using the [`xlabel`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/xlabel.html?searchHighlight=xlabel&s_tid=srchtitle_support_results_1_xlabel) and [`ylabel`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/ylabel.html?searchHighlight=ylabel&s_tid=srchtitle_support_results_1_ylabel) function
% Add your code here:

% Check Answer:
CheckLabel(gca)
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:71f7) **Exercise.**  Add a [`title`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/title.html) to your figure.
% Add your code here:

%%
%[text] The data presents a lot of outliers; you might want to change the limit of the y-axis to zoom on the box chart.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:81cc) **Try.** Modify the limit of the y-axis.
ylim([0,1600]) %[control:slider:6ec5]{"position":[7,8]} %[control:slider:2a96]{"position":[9,13]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] When you are satisfied with your figure, you might want to save or export it to your format of choice. The MATLAB `saveas` function allows you to export the figure to your preferred format.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:2720) **Try.** Export the figure with your image format of choice
if exist("h","var") 
    filename = ""+".eps"; %[control:editfield:6ae0]{"position":[16,18]} %[control:dropdown:5bd8]{"position":[19,25]}
    if extractBefore(filename,".") == ""
        warning("Please complete the edit field above to specify the file name.")
    else
        filepath = fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,"Images",filename);
        saveas(h,filepath);
    end
end
%[text] 
%[text] ![MATLAB icon](text:image:4f5c) **Pro-tip.**  Formats such as (.eps), (.svg), and (.png) are the preferred way to export your figure as an image file. The (.fig) is a MATLAB format that allows you to reopen and modify your figure later.
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9DBF4FF3] #### Removing outlier
%[text] In the previous section, we represented the data using a box chart. This figure showed the presence of numerous outliers in the results gathered from the Arduino. The outliers are any data point over the box plot's maximum notch region. The maximum edge of this region is defined using the following formula, in which $m${"altText":"m"} represents the median, $IQR${"altText":"IQR"} is the interquartile range, and $n${"altText":"n"} is the number of data points:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $m+\\frac{1.57\\ IQR}{\\sqrt n}${"altText":"m+\frac{1.57 IQR}{\sqrt n}"}
%[text] Gathering reactions can be a long process during which the participants might be inattentive. We are considering that every reaction time over one-second results from inattention. Moreover, we assume that inattention is not correlated with the participant's dominant arm. Therefore, we will remove these data points from the results.
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:2838) **Exercise.** Modify the **TimeLimit** to be 1 second (1000 ms) and the **operator** to remove the reaction time over 1 second.
TimeLimit = 2000; %[control:slider:9e3a]{"position":[13,17]}
IdxOutlier = SerialData.Data == TimeLimit; %[control:dropdown:1213]{"position":[30,32]}
NewTable = SerialData(~IdxOutlier,:);

% Visualize the out
figure
h = histogram(SerialData.Data,SeriesIndex=2,BinWidth=(TimeLimit-min(SerialData.Data))/8,BinLimits=[min(SerialData.Data) max(SerialData.Data)]);
hold on
histogram(NewTable.Data,SeriesIndex=1,BinWidth=(TimeLimit-min(SerialData.Data))/8,BinLimits=[min(SerialData.Data) max(SerialData.Data)]);
hold off
h.Parent.YAxis.Scale = "log";
xlim("tight")
xline(TimeLimit,"--")
legend("Removed datapoints","Remaining datapoints","Reaction Time limit")
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:4f57) **Try.** Plot the remaining data points in a box plot.
  %[control:button:499a]{"position":[1,2]}
h = PlotSerialData(NewTable);
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:9ca4) **Try.** Explore the mean, median, max, and min of the reaction time gathered.
if ismember("Side",NewTable.Properties.VariableNames)
    TableSummary = pivot(NewTable, Rows="Side", DataVariable="Data", Method="mean", RowLabelPlacement="rownames") %[control:dropdown:9810]{"position":[77,83]}
else
    warning("Serial data format is not formatted correctly, please follow all of the instructions.")
end
%[text] ![](text:image:9887) **Reflect.** Is your reaction time better on your right or left hand? Does it correspond to your dominant hand? (The dataset included in the module was created by a right-handed person)
%%
%[text] So far, we learned how to load and pre-process data. At this point, you might want to save the pre-processed data to use another time without going over the pre-processing steps again. The [`save`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/save.html) command allows storing any variable from your workspace in a file. 
